## UI responsiveness and React structure (March 2026)

**What we learned**

- **Context value stability:** If a context provider sets `value={{ a, b, c }}` in render, a new object is created every time, so every consumer re-renders whenever the provider re-renders—even when `a`, `b`, `c` did not change. **Fix:** Wrap the value in `useMemo` with dependencies `[a, b, c, ...]` so the reference only changes when the contents change. This is especially important when the provider wraps heavy trees (e.g. HomeScreen and a pre-mounted HabitLoggingScreen) that both subscribe to the same context.
- **List components (FlatList, SectionList):** Passing inline `renderItem`, `ListHeaderComponent`, or `ListEmptyComponent` (new function or JSX every render) makes the list think props changed and can force unnecessary row re-renders. Use `useCallback` for `renderItem` / `renderSectionHeader` / `renderItem`, and use a stable reference for header/empty (e.g. a named component or `useMemo`). For row content, extract a `React.memo` row component and pass only the props that row needs (e.g. per-habit slices like `habitLogValue` and `yesNoCounts`) so only rows whose data changed re-render.
- **Default export when adding components:** When adding a new component at the bottom of a file that already has `export default ScreenName`, do not remove or overwrite that export. If the default export is lost, other files that `import ScreenName from '...'` receive the module object instead of the component, and React reports "Element type is invalid: expected a string or a class/function but got: object" at the place that renders that component (e.g. HomeStackWrapper rendering HabitLoggingScreen).
- **Insights (or any tab) reload on focus:** Calling `setLoading(true)` and a full fetch on every `useFocusEffect` makes switching back to the tab feel slow. If we already have data (e.g. `tabData.groups.length > 0`), show it immediately and optionally refetch in the background so the spinner does not reappear on every visit.
- **Derived list data:** Building `sections` or other list input in render without `useMemo` creates a new array every render; the list then may do extra work. Compute with `useMemo` and the right dependency array (e.g. `[groups, analysisMode]`), and memoize the list header and any callbacks passed to the list so the list receives stable props.

